# Skeletons and visual loading

Mandatory. Never invent a one-off skeleton look per screen.

## Source of truth

- Contrast and fill: CSS vars `--skeleton-fill`, `--skeleton-surface-*` in `variables.scss`
- Base styles: `ion-skeleton-text`, `.skeleton-surface`, `.skeleton-circle`, `.skeleton-pill`, `.skeleton-block` in `glassmorphism.scss`

## Rules

- Prefer the global utilities. Local SCSS may set **only** width/height/layout.
- Do **not** redefine `--background`, border-radius, or glass fill for skeletons in a component.
- If Angular encapsulation blocks a global surface class on a local card (e.g. `.kpi-tile`), reuse the **same CSS variables**, not a new palette.
- Spinners (`ion-spinner`) are not a substitute for content placeholders when the layout already has cards/tiles/tables.

## Examples

```html
<!-- Correct -->
<div class="kpi-tile skeleton-surface">
  <ion-skeleton-text animated class="skeleton-circle"></ion-skeleton-text>
  <ion-skeleton-text animated></ion-skeleton-text>
</div>

<!-- Incorrect: local fill / random radius -->
ion-skeleton-text { --background: rgba(255,255,255,.05); border-radius: 10px; }
```
